  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nHigh pressure sliding southeast into the Upper Midwest behind a\ndeparting cold front will allow for a relative break in the \naction for today, and actually a very pleasant day is on tap \nwith all the synoptic factors in place. Broad subsidence, warm \nearly August temperatures and comfortable humidity levels will \nmake for quite the Saturday today, featuring highs into the \nlower 80s.\n\nHowever, as the surface high slides into the Ohio Valley later\ntoday into tonight, the aforementioned surface front will \nretreat slightly northward tonight and linger just to the south \nof MN/WI, if not make slight inroads into southern portions of \nboth states tonight through early Tuesday. The front will link \nup with a developing low pressure center over the Dakotas which \nwill then slowly slide eastward along the front over the \nweekend. In addition, broad troughing aloft will send several \nshortwave disturbances rounding the base of the trough over our \nregion during the next few days, particularly the evening- \nnighttime periods, and some of these disturbances will coincide \nwith enhanced nocturnal low-mid level jetting. The return of the\nfront will also bring a slight uptick in humidity levels, \nnothing excessive but dewpoints will rise from the 50s today to\nthe 60s to near 70 over the next couple of days. This will \nelevate instability levels which, when combined with the \nnocturnal jetting and upper level disturbances with the front \nacting as a low-level focusing mechanism, may be just enough to \nproduce scattered showers and thunderstorms over the next few \nmainly nighttime periods. It's the low-mid level jetting which \nmay be the tipping factor that could produce a few strong-to- \nsevere storms each nighttime period tonight- Monday night, \nparticularly for southwestern to southern Minnesota. Coverage \nwill be pretty sparse and uncertainty is fairly high in even the\ndevelopment of any strong-to-severe storms. However, its \npotential cannot be ignored, thus the delineations of Marginal \n(Level 1 of 5) Risks in the SWODY1, SWODY2, and SWODY3 outlooks \nby SPC.\n\nThe upper level low lingering over Hudson Bay finally loses its\ngrip Tuesday morning, opening up and shifting eastward, \nallowing the surface front lingering nearby to no longer linger \nnearby and bring in broad surface high pressure over the north-\ncentral CONUS. While a few weak disturbances aloft may drift \nthrough, the lack of any aligning surface features means few \nmentions of precipitation from Tuesday onward through the rest \nof the week. And, any mention of precip that is found in the \nforecast will have little QPF and forcing along with it. In \naddition, the warm mid-August temperatures feature in the \nforecast through Wednesday will take a tumble back for the end \nof the upcoming week as upper level flow turns from WNW to more \nNNW, bringing in a touch of cooler Canadian air which will \nreside over the area from Thursday onward. This will push highs \nfrom the 80s through Wednesday back down to the 70s starting \nThursday. Similarly, lows forecasted in the upper 50s to mid 60s\nthrough Wednesday night will drop to the 50s area-wide from \nThursday night into next weekend.",
